// One callee, two call sites, two allocators.
//
// `alloc_and_return` hands fresh storage back to its caller. The first call site
// keeps the result in `main`'s frame, so `main` creates an arena, passes the handle
// and releases the chunk on the way out. The second call site gives the storage
// away - `keep` takes ownership and releases it - so no arena can own it: that site
// passes null and the same callee body falls back to the general allocator.
//
// The callee gained the trailing handle once, and both calls supply it. Neither
// caller's decision erases the other's, which is the whole point of the nullable
// argument.
int* function alloc_and_return() {
    var node = memory.allocate<int>(8);
    node[0] = 42;
    return node;
}

void function keep(int* pointer) {
    print(pointer[0]);
}

int function main() {
    var bounded = alloc_and_return();
    print(bounded[0]);

    var escaped = alloc_and_return();
    keep(escaped);
    return 0;
}
